    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'll focus on leucine-rich sources like whey and poultry then. One more thing—some of my clients get worried about \"catabolism\" if they miss a meal or if their protein intake isn't perfect. Can you explain the actual biochemical mechanism of how the body shifts from an anabolic state to a catabolic state? I want to be able to explain the cellular breakdown process clearly to them.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The shift from an anabolic to a catabolic state is a delicate balance, often tipping toward breakdown when energy or amino acid signals are low. When you miss a meal, or if the protein supply is insufficient, the body starts to perceive a state of energy deficit, which is a trigger for catabolism. This is where the body starts to employ pathways that break down existing tissues, including muscle, to liberate amino acids for energy or to maintain vital functions.\n\nBiochemically, this involves a downregulation of anabolic signaling (like mTOR activity) and an upregulation of catabolic pathways, most notably the ubiquitin-proteasome system. This system tags muscle proteins with ubiquitin molecules, marking them for destruction by the proteasome, which essentially chops them up. It's a survival mechanism, not a desirable state for hypertrophy, which is why consistent nutrition is so crucial.",
      "source": "target",
